// Book Four, Chapter 18 (session 48). 30 July 1866: the convention meets at the Mechanics' Institute and the city turns on it. The family's morning, the noon on the Rue Dryades, the evening in the Tremé.
// Opens with no phase (the heat, the summons), a short hunger scene that only runs if must_feed() is true, then a day phase of three actions out of four (the morning), then the Institute as a climax scene, then the evening's choice, then a long dawn.
// Reads b4.circle, b4.coven_home, b4.compact, b4.peace, b4.teche, b4.honorine, b4.truth, b4.convention, b4.petition, b4.codes, b4.physician, b4.confession, b4.lucien. Sets b4.stand, b4.march, b4.troops, b4.institute and b4.aftermath.
// Ends with rest() and => next, which pauses until chapter 19 exists (the manifest entry is incomplete).
@chapter b4c18
@title The Mechanics' Institute
@version 2

# start
## The thirtieth of July
$ b4.stand = "letter"
$ b4.march = "watched"
$ b4.troops = "none"
$ b4.institute = "ran"
$ b4.aftermath = "hospital"
$ do discover("the_mechanics_institute")
~> morning
---
?if must_feed()
  ~> hunger
?end
$ do phase("day", 3)
It is not yet seven, and there are four things that want doing before noon, and time for three. {cap(voice("hunger"))}.
-> hub

# morning
## Monday
It is the hottest morning of a hot summer. There is no wind at all. The river lies flat and brown, and the smell of it comes a long way up into the town, and the iron of the balconies is warm to the hand at six.
There is something in the air that has nothing to do with the weather. It is the quiet of a city that has made up its mind. Shutters that are generally open are shut. The bars are doing a very early trade, and are not at all noisy. At the corner of the Rue Royale, a man with a long ledger face, who sells newspapers, has put away every paper but one, and is standing in front of it.
The Mayor's police have been called out in full. Their coats are buttoned in this heat. The Army, as you learned at three in the morning, from Wren, is in its barracks, below the town, and has been told that it is a civil matter.
A card is brought to the door at a quarter to seven by a boy in a gray livery, on a silver tray. It is in a very beautiful hand. It has a river house's pearl at the corner, and it says, *Monsieur Beaumont requests, with the greatest regret, that the house of the sixth blood will be so good as to receive him, or to be received, before eight. The first article.*
<~

# hunger
## The warm dark
* Go down to the stables of the Rue Dauphine, where the cab horses stand, and take what the hostler will not miss
  $ do feed("animal")
  $ humanity += 1
  It is very hot in the stable, and the horses stamp, and flick their ears, and stand for it, quite patient. A boy asleep in the straw does not wake. You stand in the doorway afterwards, in the gray first light, with a hand on the frame, and you do not at all like how still the street is.
* Send for a willing donor, and pay for the morning
  $ do feed("donor")
  $ do earn(-2)
  She is a laundress, who does the family's sheets, and she takes it with a good deal of impatience, and a great deal of grace. She says that she will be at the Institute at noon with her husband, and her two brothers, and the whole of her church. She says that she is not afraid, because there are so many of them, and goes off with the money in her glove.
* Take a man from the lobby of the hotel on the Rue Royale, who has been drinking since four, and telling the clerk what the day will bring
  $ do feed("human")
  $ humanity -= 2
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  He is a heavy man with a revolver under his coat. He will not need it. He is found in an alley at nine, quite whole, quite asleep, the revolver neatly unloaded beside him, and does not, in the course of the day, take part in anything.
